  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "The total number of eggs laid by a pen of chickens on a farm is given by the function $e(t),$ where $t$ is the number of days since the chickens arrived on the farm. The values of $e(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $e(t)$ | $20$ | $45$ | $77$ | $117$ | $160$ | $195$ |\n\nApproximate the rate at which eggs are laid at the $4$th day using a forward difference approximation.",
  "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{e}^{\u2032}(t)\u2248\\frac{e(t+h)-e(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=4.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\u2032}(4) & \u2248\\frac{e(4+1)-e(4)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e(5)-e(4)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{160-117}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{1} \\\\  & =43.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at $t=4$ the rate at which eggs are laid is approximately $43$ eggs per day.",
